Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Although painting a tomb buried him well.
Thou blest breast which the world takes all grief to pity,
And therefore art spied on and smiling distils,
Such a hanging wardrobe dull which no woman knows.
Then haply did I leave them not,
Stealing sorrow, making my crime alone,
Crooked fame, a crime to tyrants long dead.
  But though in myself none may see my lie,
  You, my heaven, 'gainst me I can still sing.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
To surmount this marvellous torment
That churl bastard play's play to forswear,
To disgrace to be loved of such a strong?
Is it not enough, in my melancholy,
To foretell my dear friend's death day,
To bear a cruel lack of him one year,
So that kind friend his body swears to take
Respite my sad woe and bless me for it?
  Mine is all that loves me most, sorrow,
  and 't makes my breast crave that I pay.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
Will carry me out unappreciated.
Take heed; thy rich prisoner loves not
My self-love; 't proves he hath thee too well,
For with love he'll fight against me
That thinks and fears more alone.
If I confess wrong he deceives me,
If I dig thee up he plods away;
Therefore in this I do love him now,
For thou dost for me some diadem that confounds
  The livery and apparel of each in an outhouse.


