And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Much more that by my deeds lie unsanctioned,
More than what is due and vows solemn.
With my murd'rous double thee I lose no pleasure,
More than my brave life when blood too strong
Plattered unprovoked my foes; my verse gives thee rest.
In it I consent that any injury doth rehearse
From some past date upon older record.
  Hence false or true lives rest on whether wit, or heart, or brain,
  Or former quality or not by me sings it.


