That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Since both thou and I have held me in envy,
Thou, having many subjects to think upon,
To blame me cannot be sweet.
Thou art the true wife to my heart,
And, I suppose, kind-hearted in thy farther ways;
Yet would I not use the word kindness
Which insults me and cold calls self esteem.
  For thy world is self-same, though ill expressed;
  For thyself the very clay grows rotten, becoming thy enemy.


