O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Thine own sweet humor consummate;
From self doth tender grace pay thy due.
Roses, roses, that bud doth in full sap behold,
But in sap lusts do not grow;
Ladies are blooms on sinews which lack sense.
And love is intelligence that doth entertain
To such humbleness, that praise and blame arise
But of mutual hate and consent.
  And humor subsists, living but in thine eyes,
  Which fools are not used to praise for love.


